[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. It is more than one problem.
The desk merged on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), and
every report says the same thing: the date is wrong. The bodies, the tenants and their booking
forms say why, and the why is not the same.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes the first row only. The one-day moves stay after it ships.
</Callout>

## The problems

**`parser`: day and month swap.** The shared parser reads every date month first, and every
tenant in #4471 writes the day first. "Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July." It reaches
every tenant through the app, email and both booking forms.

The kb files the `booking-v3` form reports apart, as `hint`. They are the same bug.
`booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, the order the parser already reads, so a person who follows
the hint gets the right date. A swapped date through that form means the person typed the day
first, as their country does, and the parser read it month first. One fix closes both.

**`one-day`: a day off, not a swap.** "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11
May." "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." These are time zone moves.
The desk already read them this way (`desk-notes.json`) but still counted them under `parser`.

## How to read the counts

- One id, one report: the export repeats some reports, so the table counts unique ids.
- The tenant decides the problem, not intake's tag. Intake's tag is a guess from the browser,
  and its `hint` tag is not a problem of its own (see the table below).
- Every number on this page is computed at render time from the export. None is typed.

